course: dictionary
lesson: 3536
title: Slang and swear words
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words are slang: rough, everyday talk that is not used in school or polite company. Context helps you spot them. «Feck» is an Irish word, milder than the f-word, used in anger or as a joke. The clue is that it is compared with a swear word, so it is not a word to copy.
explain: Knowing a word is not the same as using it. You may read or hear slang in books, on TV or from people you meet, and you can understand what it means. But at school, and in polite company, choose other words. If you are not sure, ask a grown-up.
explain: Words can also show where someone comes from. Ireland has its own slang, and in some places «feck» is heard on TV comedy. Elsewhere it sounds Irish and a bit rude. The same word can feel different in different places.
fact: Slang is informal, everyday language that is not used in school writing or polite talk.
fact: «Feck» is Irish slang, used as a mild swear word in anger or surprise.
fact: In Ireland «feck» is seen as much milder than the f-word, but it is still rude talk.
fact: Some Irish slang uses «feck» to mean to throw, as in «feck it over here».
fact: Do not use «feck» at school or in polite company, even if you hear it on TV.
exercise: «Feck» is an Irish word, milder than the f-word. What kind of word is it? | A name for a sheep | A type of cheese | *A mild swear word from Ireland | A kind of Irish dance
exercise: «Feck» is used in anger or as a joke, and it is rough slang. Where should you use it? | In a letter to your gran | In your maths test | When you read your library book aloud | *Nowhere at school or in polite company
exercise: «Granny never swears; she says fiddlesticks instead.» What does Granny say instead of swearing? | *Fiddlesticks | Bloody | Feck | The f-word
test: What does the context tell you about «feck»? | *It is rough, mild swearing | It is a kind of sweet | It is a place in Ireland | It is a school subject
test: What should you do when you meet a slang word you do not know? | Use it at school to sound clever | Shout it loudly in class | Ignore the whole text forever | *Read the words around it, then ask a grown-up or check a dictionary
exam: Which is the best reason not to use «feck» at school? | It is only used in Scotland | It is too long to spell | It means a type of cake | *It is rough slang, not for school or polite company
